{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793734805,"nonce":"/iuuzn8fJ0GdmRfOvdKKot3VKhc=","title":"Found 2026-11-04 while building 5d5fc8a. ADR-0025 says `on: upstream` starts a new, causally linked Run when another Pipeline finishes (`on: { upstream: { pipeline, status } }`, cross-repo via RBAC). Today:","message":"- the Pipeline check accepts `upstream` as a trigger key;\n- `Event::Upstream { repo, run }` exists and policy-test can build one (crates/scarab-server/src/policies.rs);\n- nothing emits it when a Run finishes, so an `on: upstream` Pipeline never fires;\n- `TriggerFilter` has only `when:` and `schedule:` with deny_unknown_fields, so `pipeline:` / `status:` as the ADR writes them fail the check.\n\nNo message queue is needed: a Run's status change already writes `run_status_changed` to the transactional outbox in the same transaction (the forge-status drainer consumes it). An upstream trigger is a second outbox kind written at the terminal transition, consumed at-least-once, deduped by a unique (upstream run, downstream pipeline) key.\n\nProposal, NOT agreed by the owner yet:\n1. Syntax `on: { upstream: { pipeline: build, status: [succeeded] } }`, status default succeeded.\n2. Same repo first: at completion list `.scarab/` at the upstream Run's commit and start each Pipeline naming it; no index. Cross-repo later (needs a listener index like cron's and an RBAC check on the downstream repo).\n3. The downstream Run pins the upstream Run's commit.\n4. Loops: each upstream-triggered Run records its chain; a Pipeline already in its chain is a refused Run.","files":null}]}